	);
};
```

### QA Notes

A lot of code was churned for this PR. I've made every possible effort to test modal React rendering, but it's possible that I missed something.

### Introduction

Positron makes use of numerous modal React components such as dialog boxes, context menus, and popups. As work progressed and new modal React components were added, it became clear that `PositronModalReactRenderer` (and the `PositronModalDialog` and `PositronModalPopup` components) were not handling keybindings properly. There were two main problems:

1) Some keybindings that should have been available in modal React components were being filtered out by the `PositronModalDialog` and `PositronModalPopup` components ('copy', 'cut', 'editor.action.selectAll', 'editor.action.clipboardCopyAction', 'editor.action.clipboardCutAction', 'editor.action.clipboardPasteAction').
2) The `IKeybindingService` was not being used to determine which keybindings should be available.

This PR addresses these problems.

### Background

When a modal React component is being displayed, an overlay DIV (normally transparent, but shown in red in this screenshot) is employed to prevent a user from being able to click on anything outside of the modal component.

<img width="1331" alt="image" src="https://github.com/posit-dev/positron/assets/853239/a8133d7f-8ba9-4af9-bfbb-dec394dc5b6c">

This PR updates how keyboard events are processed when a modal React component is being displayed. It basically walls off all but a few keybindings (as mentioned in problem 1, above) when a modal React component is being displayed.

For example, a user should not be able to access the Command Palette using ⇧⌘ P while they are in the Delete All Variables modal dialog box:

<img width="1443" alt="image" src="https://github.com/posit-dev/positron/assets/853239/e16f0f2e-bbdf-4d66-a736-88b61efcbfab">

Think of this PR as being like the red overlay DIV, but for the keyboard!

### Approach

After a lot of research, and testing, I found that we needed something similar to how `BrowserDialogHandler` handles keyboard events. In particular, see this code:

```
keyEventProcessor: (event: StandardKeyboardEvent) => {
	const resolved = this.keybindingService.softDispatch(event, this.layoutService.activeContainer);
	if (resolved.kind === ResultKind.KbFound && resolved.commandId) {
		if (BrowserDialogHandler.ALLOWABLE_COMMANDS.indexOf(resolved.commandId) === -1) {
			EventHelper.stop(event, true);
		}
	}
},
```

To facilitate this, I added a new interface called `IKeyEventProcessor` that defines a how an abstract key event processor is implemented:

```
/**
 * IKeyEventProcessor interface.
 */
export interface IKeyEventProcessor {
	/**
	 * Processes a key event.
	 * @param event The key event to process.
	 */
	processKeyEvent(event: StandardKeyboardEvent): void;
}
```

(I kept the name the same as the `keyEventProcessor` in `BrowserDialogHandler` so that its relationship to it remains clear.)

Next, I added a concrete implementation of a `IKeyEventProcessor` called `StopCommandsKeyEventProcessor`. The `StopCommandsKeyEventProcessor` uses the same technique as `BrowserDialogHandler` to filter keyboard events:

```
/**
 * Processes a key event.
 * @param event The key event to process.
 */
processKeyEvent(event: StandardKeyboardEvent): void {
	// Soft dispatch the key event so we can determine whether it is bound to a command.
	const resolutionResult = this._options.keybindingService.softDispatch(
		event,
		this._options.layoutService.activeContainer
	);

	// If a keybinding to a command was found, stop it from being processed if it is not one of
	// the allowable commands.
	if (resolutionResult.kind === ResultKind.KbFound && resolutionResult.commandId) {
		if (ALLOWABLE_COMMANDS.indexOf(resolutionResult.commandId) === -1) {
			EventHelper.stop(event, true);
		}
	}
}
```

Next, I updated the `PositronModalReactRenderer` to accept a new, optional `keyEventProcessor`, and to call that key event processor on every `keydown` event:

```
/**
 * PositronModalReactRendererOptions interface.
 */
export interface PositronModalReactRendererOptions {
	readonly container: HTMLElement;
	readonly keyEventProcessor?: IKeyEventProcessor;
}
```

```
/**
 * keydown handler.
 * @param e A KeyboardEvent that describes a user interaction with the keyboard.
 */
const keydownHandler = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {
	// Process the key event if an IKeyEventProcessor was supplied.
	if (renderer._options.keyEventProcessor) {
		renderer._options.keyEventProcessor.processKeyEvent(
			new StandardKeyboardEvent(e)
		);
	}

	// Fire the onKeyDown event.
	renderer._onKeyDownEmitter.fire(e);
};
```

Finally, I updated every instance of `PositronModalReactRenderer` to use the `StopCommandsKeyEventProcessor`.

### QA Notes

Every modal React component was touched in this PR, so a thorough regression test of modal dialog boxes, context menus, and popups will be necessary.

